EXT. FLOODED RICE PADDY - MEKONG DELTA - DAWN

A shallow bowl of silver water as far as the eye can see. Banana palms, a bamboo causeway, a collapsed dike. Mist hangs at knee height. Distant gunfire, like someone beating a rug.

SERGEANT CALLUM DRUMMOND-OKORIE (30s, tall, scarred chin, drenched in muddy water to the thighs) wades in front, rifle held high. Behind him, a hundred yards of silver, a ragged line of footprints.

CORPORAL TRAN QUOC-HARTWELL (20s, small, wiry, a radio pack on his back, a bandage on one wrist) follows, treading with exaggerated care.

CALLUM
(low)
Tran.

TRAN
Sergeant.

CALLUM
You hear that?

TRAN
Gunfire. North.

CALLUM
No. Behind it.

TRAN
Water?

CALLUM
Engines.

They freeze. Mist thickens. In the distance, a low, patient throb, barely audible.

TRAN
Boats.

CALLUM
Two. Maybe three.

TRAN
Ours?

CALLUM
Doesn't matter. We're a hundred yards from cover.

TRAN
We could go back.

CALLUM
We go back, we meet the guns.

TRAN
We go forward--

CALLUM
We meet the boats.

A beat. A bird cries, flapping across the paddy. They both duck, crouch, press low to the water. The throb comes closer.

CALLUM (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Radio.

Tran unslings the pack, thumbs the switch, holds the handset to his ear. Static.

TRAN
Nothing.

CALLUM
Try again.

TRAN
It's wet.

CALLUM
Dry it.

TRAN
With what?

CALLUM
Your shirt.

Tran pulls up his shirt and wipes the handset. He tries again. A thin voice crackles through.

RADIO (V.O.)
(distorted)
...Dagger Six...Dagger Six...

TRAN
(urgent whisper)
It's HQ!

CALLUM
Give it.

He snatches the handset, cups it, speaks without breath.

CALLUM (CONT'D)
(into radio)
Dagger Six, Dagger Six, I have two. Enemy craft inbound. Request fire on grid--

A BURST of automatic fire stitches the water. They dive. Spray everywhere. Tran lands face-first in the paddy, spits, rolls. Callum kicks off the bank, rifle up, and fires twice into the mist. Something screams. A boat engine revs.

They scramble through the shallows, slipping, hauling each other by the straps of their packs. A second burst skips across the water behind them. They splash into the reeds and drop flat, panting.

TRAN
(gasping)
That was close.

CALLUM
Too close.

TRAN
Are you hit?

CALLUM
No.

TRAN
Sergeant, your arm.

Callum looks down. A thin red thread runs from his sleeve into the water. He turns it, examines it with dull curiosity.

CALLUM
It's a scratch.

TRAN
It's bleeding.

CALLUM
Scratches do.

TRAN
Let me wrap it.

CALLUM
Later.

Tran pulls a strip of cloth from his pack anyway and ties it around Callum's arm. Callum lets him.

The engines go silent. Mist swallows the paddy. Somewhere, far away, a rooster crows.

TRAN (CONT'D)
Did they pass?

CALLUM
(listening)
They stopped.

TRAN
Why?

CALLUM
Because they're listening for us.

They hold their breath. A single bubble rises from the water beside Tran's head and pops.

CALLUM (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Move on my count. One.

TRAN
(barely audible)
Two.

CALLUM
Three.

They rise, together, and run.
